{#- Crawlers read robots.txt ONLY at the root of an address (RFC 9309; Google: "crawlers don't
    check for robots.txt files in subdirectories"). This file is the live one
    only with a custom domain — which the site has since November 2026 (grapevine.neta65.org,
    README §12, "Using your own address"): the site is the root of its address, so THIS file is
    the root robots.txt search engines read, and the Weekly link check says "Skipped" for it
    (scripts/ops/root_robots.py).
    Built as a GitHub Pages PROJECT site (https://neta65.github.io/aagrapevine/ — the fallback
    address of the Code check and of a build on a computer) it is published at
    /aagrapevine/robots.txt instead, where no search engine reads it. The file that counts there
    is https://neta65.github.io/robots.txt, and only the NETA65 account's own user-site
    repository, NETA65/neta65.github.io, can publish it: its ready-to-upload files are in
    docs/user-site/, the owner's steps in README → "Search engines" (step C), and the Weekly link
    check reads it every Sunday for such an address. Without that repository the root answers
    404, which means "crawl everything".
    So keep it as it is — everything allowed, no Disallow lines (Google needs the CSS, scripts
    and JSON files to show the pages), and the sitemap.
    The Sitemap line needs an absolute URL: site.url comes from config/site.yml (or the
    SITE_URL the GitHub Action sets). Without one it is left out. -#}
User-agent: *
Allow: /
{% if site.url %}
Sitemap: {{ site.url }}/sitemap.xml
{% endif %}
